A clip VNAS asked for plays to its end before the stage's.

The card switched to a stage's clip whenever the stage changed, also while
a clip a command asked for was loading or playing: a turn that took its next
step, or ended, right after VNAS played an animation cut that animation
short, and its command was answered cancelled (superseded). Upstream a stage
is a status the pet shows between commands, which come from a third party at
any time; in A-NAS the commands come from the turn itself, so the turn's
next step arrived during almost every animation it asked for.

While a command's clip is loading or playing, a stage change now only
updates the bubble and keeps the stage's clip, which plays at the next end
instead of the clip the chain would pick (animation-flow applyEndedCue). A
stage that goes idle meanwhile drops it, and a stage that switches at once,
or a stage clip put back by the card, replaces it. A click or a drag still
cuts a command's clip short, as upstream.

